# E6b Lazy fetch: is the low step-to-step reuse harmful? (k=5%, S=4, W=64)

**TL;DR:** count hit-rates LRU 1×/2×/4×: 0.559 / 0.726 / 0.853; *mass-weighted* hit-rates 0.771 / 0.866 / 0.928.
Attention error with lazy fetch (misses served coarse this step, fetched for the next): 0.0714 / 0.0664 / 0.0637 vs ideal 0.0606 (and 0.1450 with no dynamic residuals at all).
Lazy fetch with LRU 2× recovers 93% of the error reduction that ideal dynamic residuals give over S+W-only.

## Config
Walk-forward over 512 consecutive decode steps; per KV head LRU; capacities in units of the mean dynamic hot-set size.
Reference = exact attention on int8 cache; GS planes as in E8 (post-RoPE, RHT, 8-bit gain).

| metric | value |
|---|---|
| ideal (all hot fine) | 0.0606 |
| no dynamic residuals (only S+W fine) | 0.1450 |
| count hit LRU1× | 0.5587 |
| mass hit LRU1× | 0.7712 |
| err lazy LRU1× | 0.0714 |
| count hit LRU2× | 0.7257 |
| mass hit LRU2× | 0.8660 |
| err lazy LRU2× | 0.0664 |
| count hit LRU4× | 0.8528 |
| mass hit LRU4× | 0.9282 |
| err lazy LRU4× | 0.0637 |

